WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.730
1915.

2
00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:04.960
A poem bounces along
like a football chant:

3
00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:10.250
"Who's for the game, the biggest that's
played," Then conscription in 1916,

4
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:11.710
and the Somme.

5
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.350
1917.

6
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.050
A soldier treated for shell shock writes:

7
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:19.560
"What passing-bells for
these who die as cattle?"

8
00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:22.090
Same war, a few years on.

9
00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:27.710
So why did the poetry of 1914 sound
so different from that of 1918?

10
00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:31.360
In this mission, you'll
read the 1915 poem closely,

11
00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.560
and ask who it was written for.

12
00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:36.760
You'll hear Owen, and
ask who he is talking to.

13
00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:41.320
And you'll ask whether everyone
really changed their tune by 1918.

14
00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:46.570
Your mission: explain why the poetry of
1914 sounds so different from that of

15
00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:47.620
1918.
